The '''History of Sarta (1846–1899)''' covers the period from the return of the coastal territories in the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]] to the end of the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] in 1899. The era was marked by industrial expansion, coastal urban growth, educational inequality between provinces, and a cautious liberalisation of the political order that left the lords' parliament dominant. The crown remained the symbolic centre of the kingdom, but domestic affairs increasingly depended on the royal companies, provincial administration, and parliamentary bargaining.

== Postwar industrial expansion ==

The return of the coast through the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]] gave Sarta a larger territorial base and a stronger industrial frontier. The restored lands of [[Occidental Province]] brought substantial population and commercial capacity back under Sartan rule, and they quickly became the main destination for migrants leaving the southern and central countryside, especially the [[Sierra Madre]].

The most visible beneficiaries of that movement were [[Bahia Alta]] and [[Mariapolis]]. Bahia Alta expanded rapidly as a port and processing centre, while Mariapolis remained the largest city in the kingdom. Growth also spread through the returned coast more broadly, where shipping, warehousing, and light industry expanded faster than in the interior. The coast had already been the kingdom's commercial axis, and the restored territory pulled even more investment and labour toward it.

== Royal companies and mining ==

The state responded to the new economic conditions by building a network of royal companies to manage public works and industry. Between 1846 and 1880, the crown chartered a series of semi-autonomous ''compañías reales'' for tasks ranging from roadway construction to railway construction. These firms operated with considerable day-to-day autonomy, but they remained answerable to parliament and were directly controlled by lords appointed through the upper house.

The most important of these companies was the [[Compañía Real de Minaria de Sarta]], formed in 1847 to manage coal mining operations. It became the only company with direct control over mining and quickly developed into a major pillar of the Sartan economy. Coal from the [[Sierra Madre]] fed both domestic industry and Volisanian demand, making the company profitable but also politically sensitive.

The company system gave the state a way to direct industrial growth without surrendering control to private capital. At the same time, it strengthened the influence of the upper-house elite, who used the royal companies to organise investment, employment, and infrastructure on their own terms.

== Urban migration and coastal concentration ==

Industrial growth drew people out of the countryside. Many rural households, especially in the southern Sierra Madre, sent family members to the coast in search of wages, while others moved permanently into the expanding cities. The result was a steady flow of workers toward Bahia Alta, the ports, and Mariapolis.

This movement changed the social balance of the kingdom. Coastal districts and the capital increasingly dominated industrial output, while the hinterlands lagged behind. The royal companies reinforced that trend by concentrating employment, transport, and investment in the same places where the population was already growing.

== Worker unrest and parliamentary reform ==

The industrial boom also produced harsher conditions in the mines. Toxic air, dust, and unsafe work practices led to repeated stoppages in 1854, with more than fifteen interruptions in coal production over the course of the year. The protests were led by workers who were demanding safer conditions, compensation, and a greater voice in how the companies were managed.

The stoppages exposed the limits of the existing political settlement. The lords' parliament could direct the companies, but it had no popular chamber to absorb the grievances that were building up in the mines and port districts. In 1855, Sarta introduced the [[House of Commons of Sarta]] as part of a cautious liberalisation of the political system. The new chamber was elected by universal suffrage for adult men of the Petran faith, giving the kingdom a wider electoral base without surrendering control to full democracy.

The reform was intended to channel industrial unrest into parliamentary politics. Rather than allowing worker grievances to remain outside the system, the state created another body through which votes could be counted and conflicts contained. The king remained a figurehead of stability with real influence, but the lords' parliament continued to manage domestic affairs.

== Occidental autonomy and education ==

Education remained highly uneven across the kingdom. In the restored coast, schooling was comparatively secure because [[Occidental Province]] retained its own systems of education, governance, and law for fifty years after the merger. The arrangement made the province function almost as an autonomous region within Sarta: it paid taxes to the central state, but it kept local institutions that were more stable and more visible than those available in many inland districts.

That autonomy made Occidental a magnet for families seeking guaranteed schooling, and it contributed to continuing migration toward the coast. The result was a widening contrast between Occidental and the rest of the kingdom. In the interior, education remained more fragmented and less accessible, while the coast developed a more regular school system and a larger literate public.

The imbalance eventually forced the state to act. In 1883, Sarta introduced national primary school education in order to reduce the gap and extend basic schooling beyond Occidental Province. The reform reflected both practical pressure and political competition: the kingdom could not ignore a coastal province that was attracting people partly because it already offered better schooling.

== Political divisions ==

By the middle of the century, two broad political currents had become visible. The pro-business, pro-[[Volisania]] [[Liga Progressivo]] drew support from coastal merchants, company directors, and parts of the capital. It favoured closer commercial ties, weaker labour restrictions, and continued cooperation with Volisania.

Opposing it was the anti-Volisania, pro-worker [[Union del Personajes]], which drew strength from miners, rural communities, and inland districts that had borne the costs of industrial growth. It supported stronger labour protections and a more independent Sartan position. The split was clearest between the coast and the capital on one side, and the rural, mountainous, and interior districts on the other.

Over time, the divide also shaped education policy, with reformers arguing that the state's reach had to extend beyond the coastal provinces if it wanted to maintain political cohesion. By the 1880s, schooling, labour, and foreign alignment had become linked questions in parliamentary debate.

== The Second War of the Isthmus ==

The period ended in 1899 with the conclusion of the [[Second War of the Isthmus]]. Sarta sent forces to support Volisania, bringing its long-standing commercial and political alignment into open military cooperation. The war marked the end of the mid-century settlement and closed the era that had begun with the return of the coast. Alfonso II died on 8 April 1855, and [[Carlos II of Sarta]] took the throne, even as the same oligarchic parliamentary structure remained in place.

== Aftermath ==

By 1899, Sarta had become more urban, more industrial, and more politically divided than it had been in 1846. The coast had consolidated its lead, Bahia Alta had emerged as a major industrial city, the royal companies had become central to the economy, and the state had been forced to expand basic education beyond Occidental Province.

The era left Sarta with a more centralised industrial economy, a larger urban population, and a political system still dominated by elites but no longer entirely closed. It also left a deeper contrast between the provinces that had long benefited from guaranteed institutions and those that had not.

